# 19. Important Parameters of read_csv()

## usecols

Reads only selected columns.

```python
df = pd.read_csv("students.csv", usecols=["Name","Marks"])
```

---

## nrows

Reads only the first n rows.

```python
df = pd.read_csv("students.csv", nrows=100)
```

---

## skiprows

Skips specific rows.

```python
df = pd.read_csv("students.csv", skiprows=2)
```

---

## index_col

Makes a column the index.

```python
df = pd.read_csv("students.csv", index_col="Roll")
```

---

## header

Specifies which row should be treated as the header.

```python
df = pd.read_csv("students.csv", header=1)
```

---

## names

Assigns custom column names.

```python
df = pd.read_csv(
    "students.csv",
    names=["Student","Age","Marks"]
)
```

---

## sep

Changes the separator.

Default separator is comma (,).

If data is separated using "|"

Example

```text
Name|Age|Marks
```

Read it like this

```python
df = pd.read_csv("students.csv", sep="|")
```

---

## encoding

Used when the file contains special characters.

```python
df = pd.read_csv("students.csv", encoding="utf-8")
```

Sometimes

```python
encoding="latin-1"
```

---

## chunksize

Used for very large datasets.

```python
for chunk in pd.read_csv("big.csv", chunksize=10000):
    print(chunk.head())
```

Instead of loading the complete file into memory, Pandas reads it in smaller parts.

